## 1. Introduction
Unmetabolized bilirubin continues to excessively accumulate in the blood, leading to diseases, such as hyperbilirubinemia $(>17.1\upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1})$ and jaundice $(>50\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1})$ , which can cause irreversible brain dam­ age and even death [1,2]. After drug and surgical treatments, hemo­ perfusion has become the third generation strategy for blood purification and separation, effectively removing exogenous and endogenous toxins by infusing whole blood into an external hemo­ perfutor loaded with adsorbents [3–5]. Numerous materials have been applied in hemoperfusion adsorbents, including activated carbon [6], carbon nanotube/angle [7], graphene [8], molecularly imprinted poly­ mer [9], and adsorption membrane [10,11]. However, most of the ma­ terials face intractable problems, such as unsatisfactory adsorption performance and poor hemocompatibility. Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), as a novel material with rapid development in the fields of drug delivery [12], catalysis [13], separation [14–17], purification [18–20], sewage disposal [21,22], have begun to exhibit potential in the blood purification field because of their high specific surface areas and large porosities [10,23–30]. For example, Li et al. [26] studied the bilirubin removal capacity of PCN-333 and MOF-808 and found that PCN-333 exhibits the best performance with a maximum adsorption capacity for free bilirubin of as high as $1003.8~\mathrm{mg}\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . However, nearly all the MOFs studied in the present published literature, including the above examples, are in microcrystalline form, and their excellent performance in laboratory scale is difficult to translate into pilot- or industrial-scale applications [31]. MOFs have been fixed into different shapes using various adhesives, such as extrusion and granulation to overcome this problem, but this strategy inevitably compromises their crystallinity and porosity induced by pressure and then significantly decreases their
surface area [32].
The incorporation of MOFs into a hydrogel or polymer matrix has been proved be an effective strategy to solve this problem and has recently attracted the attention of researchers for a wide range of fields. It is a well-known polymer that can form hydrogels through ionotropic gelation under mild conditions. Zhu et al. work in 2016 first proposed a novel in situ growth approach for incorporating HKUST-1 into alginate substrate using different metal ion cross-linked methods, but there is no further applications studied in that work [33]. Queen et al. work in 2020 pointed out a more easy polymerization strategy to fixed various MOFs into sodium alginate (SA)-polyacrylic acid (PAA) hydrogels beads, which did not compromise MOFs’ crystallinity and porosity, and the product shows great potential in the extraction of Pb and Pd ions from real-world water samples [34]. Other literatures also reported the application of this method in sewage disposal [35], seawater desalina­ tion [36], dye removal [37], gas adsorption [38], organic pollutants adsorption [39] and pharmaceutical and personal care products [20]. As mentioned above literatures, the current application of this strategy is mainly in the industrial field, and rarely in the biomedical field. This maybe related with the biological system has complex components and high requirements for biocompatibility. MOFs alone have shown great potential for bilirubin application, but their application in this field is still at the preliminary mechanism research stage due to the powder crystal morphology. Hence, to test and verify the potential of this strategy in hemoperfusion therapy, MOFs and polymers is carefully selected in this study to screen bilirubin adsorbents from the perspective of improving adsorption performance and biocompatibility. To the best of our knowledge, this work is the first time to apply the concept of MOFs-polymer beads in the field of blood purification.
On the basis of the above discussion, this work skillfully selected polymer and MOFs, mainly considering the improvement of adsorption performance and blood compatibility. For the selection of polymers, heparin-like polymers containing abundant carboxyl and sulfonated groups with excellent blood compatibility may be the best candidates as MOFs carriers [40]. Single-component polymer gel generally has poor mechanical properties and stability [36]. Hence, this work selected three commonly used heparin-like polymers, namely, SA, PAA, and poly (ethylene imine) (PEI) (Fig. 1a), to constitute an interpenetrating poly­ mer network (IPN). An IPN is composed of more than two polymers whose strong polymer–polymer interaction can effectively improve the stability and the mechanical properties [41,42]. Among the polymers, we expect SA to fix the MOFs through a simple phase conversion method and the PAA to create rich channels to endow the adsorbate with low diffusion resistance and high mass transfer rate. Finally, the PEI rich in amino groups not only increases the stability of the product by inter­ acting with SA and PAA but also greatly improves the adsorption effi­ ciency due to the amino groups that specifically recognize bilirubin [43,44]. For the MOFs, UiO-series networks are constructed from the secondary building unit composed of a $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}$ octahedral metal node and 12 linear dicarboxylic acid organic ligands, which have been extensively researched since they were first prepared in 2008 because of their excellent chemical and thermal stability [45,46]. To screen an efficient hemoperfusion adsorbent based on MOFs, five $z\mathbf{r}$ -based UiO composed of organic ligands with different numbers of benzene rings and substituents (Fig. 1b) were selected to synthesize UiO-polymer IPN beads. It is speculated that the synergy between the selective adsorption properties of MOFs and the processability of polymers, can possibly produce a highly efficient and selective hemoperfusion adsorbent.
## 2. Experimental methods
Considering the length of the article, materials, characterization in­ struments, synthesis process of UiO and SAP products, and experiments methods including sweeping study, bilirubin remove experiments, ab­ sorption kinetic / isotherm / column absorption, hemolysis measure­ ments, anticoagulation measurements platelet, adhesion tests and cytotoxicity measurements are available in supporting materials.
2.1. Preparation of UiO66- ${\cdot}N H_{2}$ -SA-PAA-PEI beads with various $w t\%$ of $U i O66–N H_{2}$
The products were prepared through three steps:
⁤ Step one: $\mathrm{UiO}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ -SA beads were prepared by the ionic crosslinking method. SA and UiO66- ${\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ were pre-dispersed in ultra­ pure water and then mixed together (final concentrations: $\mathbf{SA}=$ $20~\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ ; UiO66- ${\mathrm{\cdotNH}}_{2}=0$ , 15, 30, 45, 60, ${75\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}}$ ). The mixture was stirred at $200~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ at room temperature. Then, the solution was dropped into a $10\ \mathrm{mg{\cdot}m L^{-1}\ C a C l_{2}}$ coag­ ulation bath at a height of $5\mathrm{cm}$ from the liquid level using a 200 $\upmu\mathrm{L}$ pipette tip. The beads were gently vibrated in the coagulation bath at $200\ \mathrm{rpm}$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ for solidification and then washed completely with water.
⁤ Step two: The freshly prepared $\mathrm{UiO}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -SA beads were immersed into a reaction solution containing $_{3\mathrm{~g~}}$ of acrylic acid (AA), $240~\mathrm{mg}$ of $N,N^{\prime}$ -methylenebisacrylamide (MBA), $60~\mathrm{mg}$ of $\upalpha$ -ketoglutaric acid, and $27~\mathrm{mL}$ of deionized water. The beads were gently vibrated in the coagulation bath at $200\mathrm{rpm}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ in the dark to interpenetrate PAA into the UiO66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH_{2}}$ -SA matrix. Then, they were washed completely with water (named UiO66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -SA-PAA).
⁤ Step three: The water on the surface of the freshly prepared UiO66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -SA-PAA beads was dried with absorbent paper. Then, the beads were irradiated with ultraviolet lamp (24 W, $365~\mathrm{{nm}}$ ) for $3\mathrm{min}$ . After irradiation, the beads were activated in a $20\mathrm{-mL}$ solution containing $40~\mathrm{mg}$ of 1-(3-Dimethylaminopropyl)-3-eth­ ylcarbodiimide hydrochloride (EDS) and $40\ \mathrm{mg}$ of N-hydrox­ ysuccinimide (NHS) for $20~\mathrm{min}$ . Subsequently, the beads were taken out and placed in a $20~\mathrm{mL}$ solution containing $800~\mathrm{mg}$ of PEI. The beads were gently vibrated in the coagulation bath at $200~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ to interpenetrate PEI. Then, the beads were hardened in $1\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{wt\%}$ glutaraldehyde (GA) solution and washed completely with water (named final product UiO66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -SAPAA-PEI). The products were freeze-dried and preserved.
## 2.2. Establishment of rabbit model of acute liver failure
All experimental animals were taken care of according to the the ARRIVE guidelines and carried out inaccordance with the National Research Council’s Guide for the Care and Use of Laboratory Animals, and all procedures performed on animals were approved by the Animal Care and Use Committee of Sichuan University.
The sample solutions with $10\%$ , $20\%$ , $40\%$ and $60\%$ volume ratio of carbon tetrachloride/olive oil were prepared and injected into rabbits by intraperitoneal injection according to the dose of $2\mathrm{mL}{\cdot}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ . About $17\mathrm{h}$ or $40\mathrm{h}$ modeling without food and water, blood was taken from ear vein for blood biochemical detection, and the content of total bilirubin was measured using Catalyst One automatic biochemical analyzer (IDEXX, USA).
## 2.3. Bilirubin removal efficiency in vivo
The adsorbent was sterilized with an ultraviolet lamp in advance and then soaked in normal saline for $^{\textrm{1h}}$ . The fixed-bed column reactor was fabricated with a stainless-steel insert and a glass tube. After wetting, SAP-3 was set in the tube with a height of $6\mathrm{cm}$ and an inner diameter of $1.5\mathrm{cm}$ , with the inlet and outlet of the tube sealed with a stainless-steel screen. All the circuit devices were filled with $0.1~\%$ sodium heparin saline in advance to prevent blood from clotting.
Male New Zealand white rabbits $(n=6$ , $3~\mathrm{kg}$ , the ratio of male to female is 1:1) were treated with general anesthesia through the intra­ venous administration of $30~\mathrm{mg/kg}$ pentobarbital sodium salt (Sigma, 57-33-0) as induction anesthesia, the intramuscular administration of $0.3~\mathrm{mg{\cdotkg}^{-1}}$ Butorphanol (MSD, A141A04) for preemptive analgesia, the intramuscular administration of $1~\mathrm{mg/kg}$ Acepromazine (Shanghai yuanye Bio-Technology Co., Ltd., 61-00-7) for sedation. When the experimental rabbit under the induction anesthesia stage, $40~\mathrm{min}$ into the surgery, the inhalation of $1\%$ Isoflurane (RWD,1902801) was per­ formed for maintenance anesthesia by a mask.
Their abdominal cavities were opened from the midline to separate and expose the 2-cm long abdominal aorta. The distal and proximal ends were clamped with an artery clamp, the artery was cut off from the middle, and the vascular stump was flushed with heparin saline. A cannula $(0.8\ \mathrm{mm}\times1.6\ \mathrm{mm})$ was inserted into the vascular stump and bound with suture. Blood was extracted from the arterial drainage tube, injected into the fixed-bed column reactor through a peristaltic pump (1 $\mathrm{\mL}{\cdot}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ , $1.5\mathrm{h})$ , and finally flowed into the venous drainage tube. To determine the total bilirubin content using a Catalyst One automatic biochemical analyzer (IDEXX, USA), $1\ \mathrm{mL}$ of blood was taken at different times via the blood collection port. Routine blood tests were conducted using ProCyte Dx (IDEXX, USA).
## 2.4. DFT simulation
Given that the synthesized MOFs are basically defective crystals [47] and to make bilirubin fully enter the pore diameter, The molecular model, that is, $\mathrm{UiO}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ crystal cell with two organic ligands missing, was constructed using Materials Studio 8.0. All structural relaxation and total energy calculations were performed through a quantum chemical method on the basis of density functional theory (DFT), as implemented in the Vienna Ab initio Simulation Package [48]. The projector augmented wave method was utilized to describe the electron–ion interaction. The exchange–correlation interaction function is the generalized gradient approximation (GGA) in the Per­ dew–Burke–Ernzerhof (PBE) functional [49], and the electron wave functions are expanded using the plane waves with an energy cutoff of $520~\mathrm{eV}$ . The DFT-D3 method of the Grimme van der Waals correction was used to account for the long-range interactions [50]. The convergence of total energy and the force between atoms were set to $10^{-5}$ eV and $0.01\ \mathrm{eV}\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-1}$ , respectively. The Brillouin zone was inte­ grated with the Gaussian smearing method by using the smearing width of $0.05\mathrm{eV}$ . All atomic positions were fully relaxed by using the conjugate gradient algorithm. The visual operation of the model was completed in the VESTA software [51].
## 2.5. Statistical analysis
All experiments were repeated at least three times and error bars were inserted in all figures. Adsorption kinetics, adsorption isotherm, Thomas and Admas–Bohart model parameters and correlation coeffi­ cient $(R^{2})$ were fitted via the OriginPro software (OriginLab Corporation, Northampton, MA 010 60 USA). All of the presented figures were also designed by OriginPro software. The differences between groups were analyzed for statistical significance using two-tailed Student’s test and one-way ANOVA. $p<0.05$ was considered statistically significant.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Materials preparation and characterization
UiO series products UiO-FA, UiO66, $\mathrm{UiO}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , UiO67, and UiO68 were synthesized and characterized according to the reported papers with minor modifications (details in $\operatorname{ESI}{\dagger}.$ ). Multifunctional microplate reader was applied to establish bilirubin standard curves of different detecting waves in PBS medium at room temperature (Fig. S1). The SEM images and the XRD spectra show that the synthesized UiO series products exhibit a uniform morphology and high crystallinity, which can be used for the preparation of the subsequent products (analyses in Figs. S2–S3). To obtain novel MOFs-polymer beads, the phase inversion method and the step-by-step interpenetrating polymer network were adopted as shown in Fig. 1(c). First, the UiO series was evenly dispersed in a sodium alginate (SA) aqueous solution. After $24\mathrm{~h~}$ of stirring, the UiO-SA mixture was dropped into a calcium chloride solution to form spherical beads, which result from the surface tension between the constituent liquid and the surrounding medium (air and liquid) [52]. The UiO was immobilized in self-assembly (SA) “egg box” networks cross-linked by calcium ions. Notably, the bilirubin was separated from the solution during the adsorption process by the UiO-SA beads (Fig. S4), which could inevitably affect the subsequent adsorption experiments. Furthermore, SA gel beads have the disadvantages of a small surface area and a dense gel layer, which may hinder the active fluid flow of the adsorbate [53].
To avoid this problem and increase the mechanical strength of the product, this study further interpenetrated PAA and PEI polymers into the SA network. Fig. S5 indicates that the removal rate of bilirubin by the UiO66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -SA-PAA-PEI beads increased gradually with the mass concentration of $\mathrm{UiO}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ . When the concentration increased to 40 $\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ , the increase of the removal rate decelerated, and the removal rate at $75~\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ was approximately 3.5 times that without UiO66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , suggesting that the MOFs in the beads contributed the most to the removal of bilirubin. Digital images of the freshly prepared products at this concentration $(75~\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1})$ are shown in the Fig. 1(c), and the morphology can basically remain unchanged after freeze-drying (Fig. S6). The prepared beads are labeled SAP-1–5 according to the determined number in Fig. 1(b). The SEM images of SAP-1–5 indicate that the gel molecule networks endowed the products with an open and interconnected porous structure to ensure effective mass diffusion and transfer. Especially for SAP-3, $\mathrm{UiO}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ maintained the initial morphology and was uniformly distributed in the networks of polymers, as proven by the cross-section image, EDS mapping images of the Zr, C, O, N, Ca elements (Fig. 1d), and the XRD pattern of SAP-3 (Fig. S7). The good distribution maybe related to the interaction between the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ group of $\mathrm{UiO}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and the PAA and SA molecules, which resulted in the immobilization of $\mathrm{UiO}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ into the polymer gel network.
However, MOFs aggregated in varying degrees in the other four beads, resulting in compromised adsorption efficiency to smaller molecules (Figs. S8–S11). The reason aggregation for SAP-1 and SAP-5 may be caused by their extremely small size $\mathrm{:<}20\:\mathrm{nm}\mathrm{\:,\:}$ . And for SAP-2 and SAP4, the crystal structure was damaged, and UiO68 even collapsed to a smaller size in the framework of the SAP-4 product, which may be related to the entry of polymer into the MOFs’ pores and its poor water stability. While for SAP-1, SAP-3 and SAP-5, the crystallinity contained almost intact.
The TGA results of the products basically present the same law (Fig. S12): the first stage $100^{\circ}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{-}150^{\circ}\mathbf{C})$ is attributed to the weight loss of water; the second stage $(200^{\circ}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{-}300^{\circ}\mathbf{C})$ is the carboxyl dehydration and decarboxylation; and the third stage $(350^{\circ}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{-}500^{\circ}\mathbf{C})$ is the degra­ dation of polymer residues and organic moiety of MOFs [54]. The me­ chanical stability test results (Fig. S13) reveal that three dry beads could withstand the weight of approximately $\boldsymbol{500}\mathrm{g}$ for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ and remain intact, avoiding secondary pollution in the adsorption process. The swelling tests of the dry beads in water or saline (Fig. S14) revealed many voids inside the beads, which allowed water to penetrate the beads, resulting in a large degree of swelling.
## 3.2. Comparison of bilirubin adsorption efficiency of SAP-1–5
A hemoperfusion adsorbent that performs with high efficiency and selectivity toward bilirubin must be urgently developed. In this work, the high porosities of MOFs and the interconnected porous structure of gel provide low diffusion resistance for bilirubin capture. As shown in Fig. 2(a), SAP-3 and SAP-5 presented approximately $70\%$ removal rate within $5\mathrm{min}$ and over $80\%$ at $30\mathrm{min}$ , whereas SAP-1, SAP-2, and SAP-4 displayed a low removal rate (lower than $50\%$ ) and a long equilibrium (approximately $120\mathrm{min}\mathrm{.}$ ). Thus, the adsorption efficiency of the product increased with the flexibility of the UiO organ ligand $\mathsf{S A P-}5>{\mathsf{S A P-}}1>$ S $\mathsf{A P-4}>\mathsf{S A P-2}_{.}^{}$ ). The collapse of the MOFs structure (see Fig. S10) in SAP-4 may be the main reason why its performance is far lower than that of SAP-5, indirectly suggesting that the MOFs in the beads contributed the most to bilirubin removal. When the ligand equipped an amino group that specifically recognizes bilirubin, the adsorption efficiency of the product can be greatly improved $(\mathsf{S A P-3}>\mathsf{S A P-2})$ ). The results above reveal that the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ groups and the flexibility of aperture are crucial to the bilirubin capture. In practical application, bilirubin is bound to HSA at a binding constant of up to $9.5\mathrm{\stackrel{-}{\times}}10^{7}\mathrm{L}{\cdot}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ [55]. Hence, the re­ ported adsorbents with high adsorption efficiency for free bilirubin generally performed greatly reduced adsorption efficiency in albuminrich environment. Fig. 2(b) shows that SAP-3 had the highest adsorp­ tion efficiency (approximately $38~\%$ at $30~\mathrm{min}\dot{}$ in albumin-rich envi­ ronment, followed by SAP-5 and SAP-1, while SAP-2 and SAP-4 presented a near-zero adsorption capacity.
SAP-1–5 all presented well stability and protein rejection properties (Figs. S15–16). Taking SAP-3 as an example, Fig. 2(c) indicated that the UiO66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ in SAP-3 did not fall off during the adsorption process given the extremely low concentration of $z\mathbf{r}$ elements in the supernatant (lower than $0.015\mathrm{\mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1})$ . In addition, BCA protein assay kit was applied to determine the adsorption of protein. As shown the blue curve in Fig. 2(c), the protein adsorption of SAP-3 reached saturation within 5 min, and the concentration of BSA at equilibrium was about 35.15 $\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ , which was much higher than that of albuminemia patients $(15{-}25~\mathrm{\mg{\cdot}m L^{-1}})$ . This excellent anti-fouling property was mainly attributed to the rich carboxyl groups on the matrix of beads, which could repel the adsorption of electronegative HSA on the electronegative surface of beads [56]. For the weight loss of the beads after the adsorption experiments, the weight change of SAP-3 before and after adsorption in BSA and FBS media were also studied, and the results indicated that the weight of single SAP-3 after adsorption showed a slight increase, which was caused by the weight of adsorbed bilirubin and unwashed protein. Fig. S17 showed that the morphology of SAP-1– 5 remained almost unchanged after the bilirubin adsorption, but the
