## 3.3. Chemical structure for SAP-3
To explore the adsorption mechanism of SAP-3 to bilirubin, the molecular structure itself must be determined. The chemical composi­ tions of SAP-3 under each synthesis step were substantiated by XPS and the FT-IR analysis. The deconvolution of the XPS spectra of $\mathrm{UiO}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ in Fig. 3(a) indicates that C1s at 284.60, 285.67, and 288.56 eV were indicative of benzene/C-C, C–N, and $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ species, respectively [52]. The N1s at $399.37\ \mathrm{eV}$ indicates the $\mathrm{\Ph{-}N H_{2}}$ group. For $\mathrm{UiO}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ SA beads, C1s spectra displayed a new peak at $286.84\ \mathrm{eV}.$ , which is the characteristic of the $\mathtt{C-O}$ group belonging to SA. The $\mathtt{C=}0$ content increased from $12.93\%$ to $27.11\ \%$ due to the abundant carboxyl group of SA, but the peak positions of all the peaks did not shift significantly, indicating no chemical reaction between SA and $\mathrm{UiO}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ . For the $\mathrm{UiO}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -SA-PAA beads, the $\mathsf{C-N}$ group overlapped with the peak of the C–O group due to the obviously decreased $\mathsf{C-N}$ content (Ph-N of $\mathrm{UiO}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{\cdot}$ ). Many carboxyl groups in PAA further increased the con­ tent of $\mathtt{C=}0$ to $31.06\%$ , and a new $0.0{=}0$ peak appeared $(289.66\mathrm{eV})$ . Moreover, a new peak at $401.42\mathrm{eV}$ in N1s spectra appeared due to the protonation of the amino group $(\mathrm{NH}_{4}^{+})$ of $\mathrm{UiO}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ . For the final UiO66- ${\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ -SA-PAA-PEI (SAP-3) beads, the peak at $286.45\mathrm{eV}$ in UiO66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -SA-PAA shifted to $285.62\mathrm{eV}$ because PEI had many $\mathsf{C-N}$ groups. Under the activation of EDS and NHS, the carboxyl groups in the SA and PAA networks reacted with the amino groups in the PEI molecules [57,58], resulting in the shift of the peak of $\mathtt{C=}0$ (288.25 eV) to 287.57 eV and a new CO-NH peak $(400.17\mathrm{eV})$ . Notably, the amidation reaction did not consume all the amino groups, and a great quantity of nitrogencontaining groups remained $(63.12~\%)$ , which was conducive to the adsorption of bilirubin.
This synthesis process was further confirmed by FT-IR (Fig. 3b). The stretching vibration peaks $\nu_{(-0-)}$ and $\nu_{(\mathsf{C}-\mathsf{O})}$ characteristic of SA appeared in $\mathrm{UiO}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -SA, and the overlap of $\nu_{(\mathsf{C}_{-}^{-}\mathsf{O}}$ of Ar-COOH) origi­ nated from $\mathrm{H_{2}B D C-N H_{2}}$ linkers with $\nu_{(\mathsf{C}=0}$ of SA) caused the peak to widen. Consistent with the XPS results, the peak attributed to $\nu_{(\mathrm{CO-NH})}$ appeared in the end product (i.e., SAP-3). Hence, the main chemical structure of SAP-3 is presented in Fig. 3(c). The amidation reaction of PEI with the PAA and SA molecules resulted in the immobilization of $\mathrm{UiO}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ into the polymer gel network.
## 3.4. Hemocompatibility evaluation of SAP-3
Excellent biocompatibility is a significant adsorbent property in medical fields. As a hemoperfusion adsorbent in direct contact with blood, the interaction between SAP-3 and blood is particularly impor­ tant. Hence, this work evaluated the cytotoxicity of the beads in vitro for human embryonic kidney cells (293 T) and human normal hepatocytes (LO2) cells (Fig. S18). Cell viabilities of $293\mathrm{~T~}$ and LO2 were nearly uninhibited by SAP-1–5 as reflected by the cell viabilities $>100~\%$ , indicating that the beads had good cytotoxicity. The hemolytic and coagulation properties of hemoadsorbents are two key prerequisites in blood purification. Materials with hemolytic risk could disrupt the red blood cells and then allow the escape of hemoglobin from damaged cells, resulting in a red solution that is visible to the naked eye. The digital image of the sample solutions shows that SAP-3 presented nearly no hemolysis effects on erythrocytes, and the calculated hemolysis ratio was approximately $0.30\%$ , which is lower than the defined safe level of $5\%$ according to ISO document 10 993-5 1992 (Fig. 4a) [59].
The clotting times, namely, APTT, PT, and TT, were tested to eval­ uate the coagulation functions in vitro of SAP-3. APTT, PT, and TT can be used to determine the inhibitory effect on the common/intrinsic plasma coagulation pathway, the extrinsic pathway, and the time for the con­ version of fibrinogen into fibrin, respectively [60]. The length of coag­ ulation time is closely related to thrombus formation. In general, the longer the coagulation time is, the lower the possibility of thrombus formation is. As shown in Fig. 4(b), SAP-3 prolonged the clotting times, especially for the APTT (increased from 29.40 s to 42.77 s), compared with the control group, indicating that SAP-3 could affect the activity or amount of thrombin in the coagulation pathway. This anticoagulant effect may be attributed to the heparin-like properties of sodium algi­ nate and polyacrylic acid, which can effectively inhibit blood coagula­ tion and prevent the risk of thrombosis. In addition, SAP-3 presented a negligible turbulence on the FIB concentration, further proving that the probability of coagulation reaction is low.
Given the modifiability of the morphology of the gel matrix struc­ ture, a product with a thin film morphology was prepared (Fig. 4c) to determine the platelet adhesion function of SAP-3. Fig. 4(b) shows that the surface of SAP-3 without adherent platelets was still uneven in the microscopic perspective, which is related to the uneven crosslinking of hydrogels in the ionic gelation process [36]. Compared the activated platelet adhesion images on the surface of glass (Fig. 4e) and SAP-3 (Fig. 4f), only a single platelet adhesion was observed in one field of vision (in the arrow direction) on the SAP-3 surface, indicating that SAP3 was resistant to platelet mediated thrombosis. The good cytotoxicity and hemocompatibility of SAP-3 ensures its safe use as a hemoadsorbent.
## 3.5. Adsorption capacity of bilirubin by SAP-3
## 3.5.1. Adsorption kinetics and isotherm of bilirubin by SAP-3
Two kinetic models, namely, pseudo-first-order and pseudo-secondorder, were applied to analyze the adsorption kinetic behaviors of products toward bilirubin. SAP-3 rapidly adsorbed bilirubin within the initial $15\mathrm{min}$ , and then the increase of the adsorption rate decelerated because most of the binding sites of the adsorbent were occupied (Fig. 5a). Figs. 5(b–c) and Table 1 illustrate that the fit of the kinetic dates to the pseudo-second-order $(R^{2}=0.9983)$ was greater than that to the pseudo-first-order $(R^{2}=0.8501\mathrm{\Omega}$ for the SAP-3 system, indicating that this adsorption mechanism was mainly a chemical process [61].
Furthermore, two adsorption isotherm models, namely, the Lang­ muir and Freundlich models, were used to study the adsorption capacity of SAP-3 toward bilirubin. Fig. 5(d) shows that the bilirubin uptake of SAP-3 increased with the bilirubin equilibrium concentration because high concentration could promote the driving force and the mass transfer from the liquid to the solid phase. Obviously, the isotherm dates fitted more to the Langmuir model $(R^{2}=0.9914)$ than to the Freundlich model $(R^{2}=0.8790)$ for the SAP-3 system, indicating that this adsorp­ tion mechanism was mainly a chemical monolayer adsorption process with limited similar binding sites and no interaction between the adsorbed bilirubins [30]. The adsorption isotherm parameters of SAP-3 are summarized in Table 1. The maximum adsorption capacity $(q_{m})$ of SAP-3 reached up to $63.97\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ as calculated by the Langmuir model, which is higher than that calculated by any of the other four products (Table S2). The separation factor $(R_{L})$ of SAP-3 in the linear Langmuir model was $0.01~\mathrm{L}{\cdot}\mathrm{mg}^{-1}$ , which is in the range of 0–1, and the values of $1/n$ fitted by the Freundlich model were in the range of $0.5–1$ , proving that SAP-3 is a favorable absorbent to bilirubin [62].
## 3.5.2. Adsorption of bilirubin by SAP-3 under dynamic conditions
The analysis of the breakthrough curve and the corresponding mathematical model can provide important information for optimizing the adsorption column to achieve maximum capacity and satisfactory removal efficiency at the required flow rate in a large-scale treatment system from a practical view. The adsorption capacity of bilirubin by SAP-3 under dynamic conditions was determined in a fixed-bed column (Fig. 6a). The weight of the dried adsorbent was approximately $0.34~\mathrm{g};$ the bed high was approximately $1.2~\mathrm{cm}$ , and the flow rate was 0.12 $\mathrm{L}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ . After adsorbing bilirubin, the color of the SAP-3 beads changed from light brown to bright yellow, and the excellent mechanical prop­ erties ensured that the beads maintained the initial morphology after 3 h. As shown in Fig. 6(b), the breakthrough curve reached the penetration point $(C_{t}=5\ \%\ C_{O})$ before $5\mathrm{min}$ , suggesting that the bilirubin solution could permeate the beads completely and reached the exhaustion point $(C_{t}/C_{0}=98\ \%)$ after $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ of dynamic adsorption.
Detailed kinetic parameters were obtained via two widely used fixedbed column adsorption models, namely, the Thomas and Admas–Bohart models, which can estimate the maximum adsorption capacity and the adsorption rate constant. The Thomas model obeys the Langmuir and the second-order reversible reaction kinetics but is not limited to the chemical reaction between the adsorbent and the substrate. The Admas–Bohart model is based on the assumptions that the equilibrium is not instantaneous and the capacity of the adsorbent and the concen­ trations of the substrate directly affect the adsorption rate [37]. As illustrated in Fig. 6c–d and Table 2, the Thomas model predicted the breakthrough curve with a higher correlation $(R^{2}=0.961\$ ) than the Admas–Bohart model $(R^{2}=0.867)$ . The results of the fixed-bed column adsorption are consistent with those of the batch experiments, indi­ cating that SAP-3 mainly captured bilirubin via chemical adsorption. Notably, the maximum adsorption amount here fitted by the Thomas model in column adsorption $(17.57~\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ was lower than that in the batch adsorption $(63.97~\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ , implying that the bilirubin solution continuously flowed away from the surface of the SAP-3 beads before reaching the saturation state. Notably, the concentration, flow rate, quality, and size of the adsorbate affected the breakthrough curve and then the maximum/saturated adsorption capacity of the column adsorption [63]. Further optimizing the fixed-bed column can effec­ tively enhance the saturated adsorption capacity, which our group will focus on in future research. Forming the product into beads to satisfies the requirements of whole blood perfusion and has attracted the atten­ tion of researchers in recent years (Table S3). The adsorption rate of bilirubin by SAP-3 was significantly improved compared with the re­ ported adsorbent beads, which is beneficial to shortening the treatment time and avoiding side effects. Notably, despite the extremely high removal rate of SAP-3 toward bilirubin (Fig. S21), it showed a low $q_{e}$ value here because of the large weight of SAP-3 itself $(m_{\mathrm{each,dry}}=1.717$ mg). The superiority of SAP-3 is also reflected in the high adsorption efficiency for ALF animal models as discussed later. Existing research basically stops at in vitro static adsorption research, but comprehensive adsorption performance and biocompatibility investigation combined with animal experiments and even medical clinical experiments are essential to promote the realization of the practical application value of novel hemoadsorbents.
## 3.5.3. Bilirubin adsorption in rabbit models of SAP-3
Given the excellent bilirubin removal efficiency in both batch and dynamic conditions in vitro and the great biocompatibility, the good performance of SAP-3 in actual clinical treatment can be expected. Rabbit was selected as the animal model to determine SAP-3’s perfor­ mance in mimic clinical hemoperfusion therapy. First, the rabbit model of ALF was established via a carbon tetrachloride inducer. Table S4 in­ dicates that the optimal molding conditions were $60\%$ carbon tetra­ chloride volume ratio and $^{17\mathrm{~h~}}$ of incubation time, where the concentration of the total bilirubin in rabbit blood increased the most $(3.3\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}.\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ to $13.5\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}.$ .
Fig. 7(a) shows the circuit for the whole blood hemoperfusion using SAP-3 as adsorbents in vivo. Notably, the total blood volume and the actual clinical treatment device were different from the scheme designed here, so the treatment effect on humans may differ from that in this study [8]. As shown in Fig. 7(b), the comparison of the adsorption capacities of bilirubin in the control group (without beads) and the experimental group in vivo shows that SAP-3 can effectively reduce the total bilirubin index from $11.25\upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ to $7.5\upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ in the blood of the ALF rabbit model and reach the adsorption saturation after $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ (reduced to $6.5\upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ , approximately $42\%$ . In the control group, the total bilirubin index in the model blood was basically unchanged during the circuit. The composition of blood is complex, including plasma, blood cells, chromosomes, inorganic salts, cell metabolites, hormones, and enzymes. The in vivo adsorption results indicate that SAP-3 had high selectivity for bilirubin and could competitively adsorb bilirubin from albumin molecules, effectively reducing the content of bilirubin in blood. The influence of SAP-3 beads on blood components after a $1.5\mathrm{h}$ circuit was investigated through blood routine assay. Table 3 shows that SAP-3 did not present obvious impacts on the quantities of red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets, proving again that SAP-3 has good hemocompatibility as a hemoadsorbent.
## 3.6. Binding forces analysis of SAP-3 adsorbing bilirubin
Both batch and fixed-bed column in vitro adsorption mechanism studies imply that the capture of bilirubin by SAP-3 mainly exhibit the chemisorption mechanism, which may be related to the abundant amino groups, aromatic rings, and metal nodes in the bead’s matrix structure. The adsorption results in vivo also prove that SAP-3 has high selectivity and efficient removal capacity for bilirubin. Excellent adsorption per­ formance depends on the unique structure of the adsorbent and tailored binding sites. Computer simulation has become a powerful method to study the host-guest recognition patterns of macromolecules and small molecules [64]. Therefore, this work further adopted experiment and DFT simulation methods to identify the active adsorption sites and the binding force at the molecular level. Fig. 8a shows that after adsorption, the characteristic peak $(1445~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}.\$ ) of bilirubin appeared in the FT-IR spectrum of SAP-3, indicating an interaction between them. As shown in the XPS spectra in Fig. 8(b), the $\mathtt{C=}0$ group (287.57 eV) and $\mathrm{{NH}}_{2}$ group (398.95 eV) contents decreased from $13.63~\%$ to $11.23~\%$ and $63.12\ \%$ to $55.32\%$ , respectively. Meanwhile, the peak of the CO-NH group shifted from $400.17~\mathrm{eV}$ to $399.04\ \mathrm{eV}$ with the increasing con­ tent from $19.57\%$ to $22.28\%$ . The above results reveal the existence of electrostatic force between the amino group in the SAP-3 matrix and the carboxyl group of bilirubin. Furthermore, the cationic group in the skeleton also adsorbed bilirubin through electrostatic interaction, which can be deduced from the increase of the $\mathrm{NH}_{4}^{+}$ (401.28 eV) content from $17.31\ \%$ to $22.39\ \%$ . Specifically, the peak position and content of the deconvolution peak of UiO66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ ’s Zr3d (Fig. S22) nearly maintained its original state, suggesting that the $z\mathrm{r}$ atom did not participate significantly in this adsorption process.
Notably, the MOFs in the beads contributed the most to the removal of bilirubin on the basis of the discussion in 3.1. Materials preparation and characterization. Given that synthesized MOFs are basically defective crystals [47], this work constructed a UiO66- $\mathbf{\cdotNH}_{2}$ crystal cell with two organic ligands missing and studied its adsorption model with bilirubin via DFT simulation. The results reveal that bilirubin molecules could completely enter the cavity (Fig. S23a), and the binding energy between them was $-76.14\mathrm{Kcal}{\cdot}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ (Fig. 8c). The carbonyl oxygen atom on the pyrrole rings (NO.1 and NO.4 in Fig. S23b) of bilirubin and the hydrogen atom on the $z_{\mathrm{r_{6}}}$ nodes of $\mathrm{UiO}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ formed hydrogen bonds with lengths of $1.92\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}$ and $1.63\mathrm{~\AA},$ , respectively. Specifically, the carboxyl oxygen atom of the pyrrole ring (NO.3) also formed a hydrogen bond $(1.{\overset{\cdot}{98}}{\overset{\circ}{\operatorname{A}}})$ with the amino group on the side chain of the organic ligand. Fig. 8(d) shows that the two pyrrole rings (NO.1 and NO.4) of bilirubin were parallel to the benzene rings of the organic ligand at a reasonable distance for $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions (3.63 and 3.51 Å).
To summarize, the amino group in the matrix of SAP-3 contributed greatly to the recognition and adsorption of bilirubin via electrostatic force and hydrogen bonding. Furthermore, the abundant $\pi$ electrons in the $\mathrm{UiO}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ skeleton can promote the adsorption through $\pi{-}\pi$ in­ teractions, whereas the coordinatively unsaturated sites (Zr atom) contributed less to adsorption [27]. As illustrated in Fig. 8(e), combined with the study of adsorption mechanism in vitro and in vivo, we specu­ lated that the synergistic effect of polymer and MOFs realized the highefficiency adsorption capacity of SAP-3 for bilirubin. The calcium ioncrosslinked polymer IPN-gel matrix network endowed the beads with abundant and sufficiently large aperture and channel (see Fig. 1d) that allowed the HSA-bilirubin complex $(4\mathrm{nm}\times14\mathrm{nm})$ to come in contact with the binding site inside the beads with low diffusion resistance and high mass transfer rate. In general, the adsorption process is mainly divided into two steps: the interaction between bilirubin and the adsorption sites, and the self-aggregation of bilirubin molecules on the surface of the adsorbent due to hydrogen bond and hydrophobic inter­ action [65]. The abundant amino and aromatic groups in the structure of $\mathrm{UiO}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ interacted with bilirubin and competed for bilirubin out of albumin through electrostatic force, hydrogen bonding, and $\pi{-}\pi$ in­ teractions. Meanwhile, the amino group introduced by PEI in the IPN could also effectively adsorb free bilirubin in the blood, considering the specific recognition effect of the amino group on bilirubin. Bilirubin $(1.7\ \mathrm{nm}\times1.2\ \mathrm{nm})$ entered into the pore of $\mathrm{UiO}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and the steric hindrance made the adsorbed bilirubin firmly be fixed in the pore, occupying space. Therefore, the remaining bilirubin tended to selfaggregate in the channels formed by the polymer in the beads.
